'''''Zingiber''''' is a genus of flowering plants in the family [[Zingiberaceae]]. It is native to [[China]], the [[Indian subcontinent]], [[New Guinea]], and [[Southeast Asia]], especially [[Thailand]].<ref name="nutmeg">{{cite web |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:327297-2 |title=''Zingiber'' Mill. |date=2025 |website=Plants of the World Online |publisher=Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew |access-date=4 March 2025 }}</ref><ref>{{cite book |chapter-url=http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=2&taxon_id=135325 |chapter= ''Zingiber'' Miller<!-- FoC title: v 24 p 323, <big>姜属</big> jiang shu -->,|title=[[The Gardeners Dictionary|Gardener's Dictionary Abridged]] |edition=4th |volume=3 |page=1754 |via= Flora of China}}</ref><ref>Govaerts, R. (2004). World Checklist of Monocotyledons Database in ACCESS: 1-54382. The Board of Trustees of the Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew.</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Tripathi |first1=S. |last2=Singh |first2=K.K. |year=2006 |title= Taxonomic revision of the genus ''Zingiber'' Boehm. in North-East India |journal= Journal of Economic and Taxonomic Botany |volume=30 |page=520–532}}</ref> It contains the true [[ginger]]s, plants grown the world over for their culinary value. The most well known species are ''[[Ginger|Z. officinale]]'' and ''[[Myoga|Z. mioga]]'', two garden gingers. The genus name comes from Latin borrowing the [[Tamil language|Tamil]] name for the first species.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Ravindran |first1=P. N. |title=The Encyclopedia of Herbs and Spices |date=28 December 2017 |publisher=CABI |isbn=978-1-78064-315-1 |page=398 |url=https://www.google.com.my/books/edition/The_Encyclopedia_of_Herbs_and_Spices/6pJNDwAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=zingiber+taxonomy+greek&pg=PA397&printsec=frontcover |language=en}}</ref>

==Culinary==
Each ginger species has a different culinary usage; for example, [[myoga]] is valued for the stem and flowers. Garden ginger's [[rhizome]] is the classic [[spice]] "ginger", and may be used whole, candied (known commonly as crystallized ginger), or dried and powdered. Other popular [[ginger]]s used in cooking include [[cardamom]] and [[turmeric]],<ref name=kt>{{cite web |url=http://www.ktropicals.com/flowers/ginger.shtml |title=Flower notebook: ''Zingiber'' |publisher=Kalani Tropicals |access-date=November 11, 2011 }}{{Dead link|date=September 2025 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref> though neither of these examples is a "true ginger" – they belong to different genera in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Zingiberaceae]].

